{"info": {"author": "Kevin Musgrave", "author_email": null, "bugtrack_url": null, "classifiers": ["Development Status :: 5 - Production/Stable", "Intended Audience :: Science/Research", "Operating System :: OS Independent", "Programming Language :: Python :: 3", "Programming Language :: Python :: 3 :: Only", "Programming Language :: Python :: 3.12", "Programming Language :: Python :: 3.13", "Programming Language :: Python :: 3.14", "Topic :: Scientific/Engineering :: Artificial Intelligence", "Typing :: Typed"], "description": "![PyTorch Metric Learning](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/KevinMusgrave/pytorch-metric-learning/master/docs/imgs/Logo2.png)\n\nTrain embedding models with composable PyTorch losses, hard-example miners,\ndistances, reducers, and regularizers. Use them in your own training loop for\nretrieval, clustering, recognition, or learning from pairs and triplets.\n\n[Docs for humans](https://kevinmusgrave.github.io/pytorch-metric-learning/) \u00b7\n[Documentation source](https://github.com/KevinMusgrave/pytorch-metric-learning/blob/master/docs/index.md)\n\n[![How embeddings flow through miners, losses, reducers, and retrieval metrics.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/KevinMusgrave/pytorch-metric-learning/master/docs/imgs/architecture.svg)](https://kevinmusgrave.github.io/pytorch-metric-learning/imgs/architecture.svg)\n\n## Install\n\nInstall from PyPI with uv or pip:\n\n```sh\nuv pip install pytorch-metric-learning\npip install pytorch-metric-learning\n```\n\nThe core package needs only PyTorch and NumPy. Optional extras add the Faiss\nretrieval backend, `pytorch-metric-learning[faiss]`, and scikit-learn for the\nNMI/AMI clustering metrics, `pytorch-metric-learning[clustering]`.\n\nVersion 3 requires Python 3.12 or newer, PyTorch 2.14.0 or newer, and NumPy\n2.5.3 or newer. Upgrading from version 2? Read the\n[release notes](https://kevinmusgrave.github.io/pytorch-metric-learning/release-notes/3.0.0/) and look up old names in the\n[migration guide](https://kevinmusgrave.github.io/pytorch-metric-learning/migration-2-to-3/). The\n[version 2 documentation](https://kevinmusgrave.github.io/pytorch-metric-learning/v2/) remains available.\n\n## A minimal training step\n\nYour model produces one embedding per input. Equal labels identify positive\npairs; different labels identify negatives. Here a contrastive loss pulls\npositive pairs together and pushes negatives apart:\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom pytorch_metric_learning import losses\n\ntorch.manual_seed(17)\nmodel = torch.nn.Linear(8, 4)\noptimizer = torch.optim.SGD(model.parameters(), lr=0.1)\ndata = torch.randn(12, 8)\nlabels = torch.arange(4).repeat_interleave(3)\nloss_func = losses.ContrastiveLoss(neg_margin=1.4)\n\noptimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\nloss = loss_func(model(data), labels)\nloss.backward()\noptimizer.step()\n```\n\nThe model returns embeddings shaped `(12, 4)` and labels have shape `(12,)`.\nThis loss L2-normalizes embeddings by default. It averages active positive and\nnegative pair penalties separately, then adds them. See the\n[contrastive-loss contract](https://kevinmusgrave.github.io/pytorch-metric-learning/losses/contrastive_loss/#contrastiveloss) for distances,\nmargins, and reduction options.\n\n## Learn more\n\nThe [documentation](https://kevinmusgrave.github.io/pytorch-metric-learning/) starts with a task index and a\n[concepts overview](https://kevinmusgrave.github.io/pytorch-metric-learning/concepts/) of embeddings, tuples, distances, loss\nterms, and reducers. Every component page states its signature, defaults,\ntensor contract, and paper, and the [recipes](https://kevinmusgrave.github.io/pytorch-metric-learning/recipes/training/) show\ncomplete training and evaluation code.\n\n## Contributing\n\nSee [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/KevinMusgrave/pytorch-metric-learning/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md).\nThanks to all [contributors, upstream projects, and supporters](https://github.com/KevinMusgrave/pytorch-metric-learning/blob/master/CREDITS.md).\n\n## Citing this library\n\nIf you use this library in your research, please cite the\n[PyTorch Metric Learning paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.09164):\n\n```bibtex\n@article{Musgrave2020PyTorchML,\n  title={PyTorch Metric Learning},\n  author={Kevin Musgrave and Serge J. Belongie and Ser-Nam Lim},\n  journal={ArXiv},\n  year={2020},\n  volume={abs/2008.09164}\n}\n```\n", "description_content_type": "text/markdown", "docs_url": null, "download_url": null, "downloads": {"last_day": -1, "last_month": -1, "last_week": -1}, "dynamic": ["License-File"], "home_page": null, "keywords": "metric learning, deep learning, pytorch, embeddings, retrieval", "license": null, "license_expression": "MIT", "license_files": ["LICENSE", "THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md"], "maintainer": null, "maintainer_email": null, "name": "pytorch-metric-learning", "package_url": "https://pypi.org/project/pytorch-metric-learning/", "platform": null, "project_url": "https://pypi.org/project/pytorch-metric-learning/", "project_urls": {"Changelog": "https://kevinmusgrave.github.io/pytorch-metric-learning/release-notes/", "Documentation": "https://kevinmusgrave.github.io/pytorch-metric-learning/", "Homepage": "https://github.com/KevinMusgrave/pytorch-metric-learning", "Issues": "https://github.com/KevinMusgrave/pytorch-metric-learning/issues", "Source": "https://github.com/KevinMusgrave/pytorch-metric-learning"}, "provides_extra": ["faiss", "clustering"], "release_url": "https://pypi.org/project/pytorch-metric-learning/3.0.3/", "requires_dist": ["numpy>=2.5.3", "torch>=2.14.0", "faiss-cpu>=1.15.1; extra == \"faiss\"", "scikit-learn>=1.9.1; extra == \"clustering\""], "requires_python": ">=3.12", "summary": "Composable metric learning losses, miners, distances, samplers, and retrieval metrics for PyTorch.", "version": "3.0.3", "yanked": false, "yanked_reason": null}, "release_dates": {"0.9.0": "2019-10-24T20:41:35.927874Z", "0.9.1": "2019-10-24T21:09:09.457128Z", "0.9.11": "2019-10-25